1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,200
In this town of just 1,903 people, a retired couple found a way to beat the lottery.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:07,033
Legally.

3
00:00:07,033 --> 00:00:08,867
For 9 years.

4
00:00:08,867 --> 00:00:15,467
In 2003, retired store owner Jerry Selbee read a brochure for a new Michigan game, Winfall.

5
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:17,833
He found the loophole in 3 minutes.

6
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:23,200
When the jackpot hit $5 million and nobody won, the money rolled down to the smaller prizes.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,400
On those days, enough tickets put the math on your side.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:27,467
Jerry tried it.

9
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:29,567
$3,600 in.

10
00:00:29,567 --> 00:00:31,233
$6,300 back.

11
00:00:31,233 --> 00:00:37,967
He and his wife Marge formed a company with family and friends, and played 12 roll-downs, until Michigan ended the game.

12
00:00:37,967 --> 00:00:40,467
Then they found a similar game in Massachusetts.

13
00:00:40,467 --> 00:00:46,900
For 6 years, they drove 900 miles a trip, often betting over $600,000.

14
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:47,867
They weren't alone.

15
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:50,733
A group started by MIT students played it too.

16
00:00:50,733 --> 00:00:55,700
In 2011, the Boston Globe broke the story, and many suspected a rigged game.

17
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:57,133
The state investigated.

18
00:00:57,133 --> 00:00:59,967
The betting was legal, and no one's odds were hurt.

19
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:03,700
The state itself made $120 million from it.

20
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:10,433
In all, their company won over $26 million, nearly $8 million of it profit before tax.

21
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:11,867
Theirs went to family.

22
00:01:11,867 --> 00:01:14,000
Sometimes the edge isn't luck.

23
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:15,500
It's reading the rules.
